{"answers":[{"question_id":"Q003_actin_like_atpase_divergence","answer":"SCOPe places the catalytic (nucleotide-binding) domains of actin/Hsp70, hexokinase and glycerol kinase in the SAME SUPERFAMILY but DIFFERENT FAMILIES. All sit in class c (alpha/beta proteins), fold c.55 'Ribonuclease H-like motif' (3 layers alpha/beta/alpha; mixed 5-stranded sheet, order 32145, strand 2 antiparallel), superfamily c.55.1 'Actin-like ATPase domain', which SCOPe annotates as a duplication: each catalytic domain contains two RNase H-like lobes (subdomains) that close around ATP in the interlobe cleft. Within c.55.1 the three groups are separate families: c.55.1.1 Actin/HSP70 (actin, Arp2/3, Hsc70/DnaK nucleotide-binding domains, plus bacterial actin homologues MreB, ParM, FtsA; e.g. actin d1atna_/d1j6za_, Hsc70 NBD d1hpma_/d3hsca_), c.55.1.3 Glycerol kinase (E. coli GlpK, e.g. d1glaa_/d1glfa_), and c.55.1.4 Hexokinase (yeast hexokinase 1hkg/2yhx, Schistosoma 1bdg, human hexokinase I 1cza where each tandem half is a separate domain), with sibling families such as c.55.1.5 Glucokinase, c.55.1.2 BadF/BadG/BcrA/BcrD-like, c.55.1.6 Acetate kinase-like, c.55.1.7 Ppx/GppA phosphatase, c.55.1.8 YeaZ-like, c.55.1.9 Fumble and c.55.1.10 ROK. These are the 'sugar kinase/Hsp70/actin superfamily' of Bork, Sander and Valencia (1992), and the assignment is stable across SCOPe 2.0x releases (2.06, 2.07, 2.08).

What the superfamily relationship permits you to infer: (1) Probable common ancestry (homology). SCOPe superfamily membership asserts that the shared structural core (two duplicated beta-beta-beta-alpha-beta-alpha-beta-alpha lobes forming a deep cleft) plus shared functional features make a common origin likely even though pairwise sequence identity between families is at or below the twilight zone (typically 10-20%). (2) Conserved nucleotide-binding architecture and catalytic strategy: the five sequence/structure motifs (phosphate 1, phosphate 2, adenosine, connect 1, connect 2) lie at equivalent positions, ATP binds in the interlobe cleft with Mg2+ coordinated by the phosphate-binding loops, and all share induced-fit domain closure on substrate/nucleotide binding, which explains why actin's conformational cycle, Hsp70's allosteric cycle and the kinases' substrate-induced closure are mechanistically related. (3) The relationship is therefore usable for structural-core alignment, for detecting new remote members (MreB, ParM, FtsA, YeaZ/Kae1, pantothenate kinase etc. were recognised this way), and for mapping nucleotide-binding residues between members.

What it does NOT permit: (a) Function transfer. Different families mean specific function is not transferable: actin polymerises and hydrolyses ATP slowly, Hsp70 NBDs are allosteric regulators of a substrate-binding domain, hexokinase phosphorylates glucose, glycerol kinase phosphorylates glycerol and is allosterically regulated by FBP and IIAGlc. Substrate-binding residues, the second-lobe insertions (actin subdomains 2 and 4, Hsp70's IIB subdomain, the kinase sugar-binding inserts) and oligomerisation interfaces are family-specific and cannot be inferred from the superfamily. The phosphoryl acceptor (water vs. sugar/glycerol hydroxyl) and even the catalytic base differ, so 'kinase' activity must not be assumed for an Actin/HSP70 family member or vice versa. (b) Fold-level relatives are not inferred homologues: other c.55 superfamilies (c.55.3 Ribonuclease H-like: RNase H, RuvC, integrase/transposase cores, exonuclease domains; c.55.2 creatinase/prolidase N-terminal domain; c.55.4 translational machinery components; c.55.5/c.55.7 methylated-DNA-protein cysteine methyltransferase domain; c.55.6 MutS domain II) share only the topology, which SCOPe treats as possibly convergent, so no ancestry or ATP-binding inference extends to them. (c) Template use: a hexokinase or glycerol kinase structure is a usable template for an actin/Hsp70 member (or vice versa) only for the core RNase H-like lobes; sequence-based homology modelling across families is unreliable at this identity, loops, inserts and the exact interlobe hinge angle must come from a within-family template, and domain-closure state (open apo vs. closed ATP-bound) must be matched. (d) Representative selection: choose representatives at the family level, not one per superfamily, using the ASTRAL 40%-identity subsets, and remember that in multi-lobe kinases (human hexokinase I, Hsp70 full-length structures) SCOPe splits the chain into several domains, so pick the correct domain identifier rather than the whole chain. Experimental evidence (mutagenesis of phosphate-loop residues, nucleotide-bound structures, substrate specificity assays) is still required to confirm any transferred catalytic role.","evidence_used":["pretrained model knowledge","SCOPe classification hierarchy (class c, fold c.55 Ribonuclease H-like motif, superfamily c.55.1 Actin-like ATPase domain, families c.55.1.1 Actin/HSP70, c.55.1.3 Glycerol kinase, c.55.1.4 Hexokinase)","Bork, Sander & Valencia 1992 sugar kinase/Hsp70/actin superfamily (recalled from pretrained knowledge)"]}]}
